It also has dependency directives and a dedicated URL directive:
JavaScript:
/*:
@target MV MZ
@base <pluginID>
@orderAfter <pluginID>
@orderBefore <pluginID>
@url <webURL>
*/
Other than that, there's the fact that
@require
and
@noteRequire
aren't needed in MZ (but I'd include them anyway!) and the method of defining plugin commands. (Speaking of
@noteRequire
and the related directives for defining note tags for Exclude Unused Files – that's not covered in Robro's link, but you can find it in the official documentation from the Help menu.)
The source I use is
here
. It's in Japanese, but automatic translation should be mostly sufficient – just beware that it mangles the actual code.
It would definitely be helpful if someone were to gather all this information in one place though…

Managed to get it working after a lot of headaches, trials and errors
(a friend also helped me understanding parts of the code through outside examples (like how RetroAchievement's system is coded!))
Learned a lot... What helped the most was analyzing previous plugins I had from various source. Displaying them in Telegram
(with monospace formatting)
has all the code looking much easier to parse through, like if using Notepad++.
So if you set two  @ help, the previous one is overwritten. You can also make an "IF"-like description for various languages.
You can name values differently inside the 'comment'
(word?)
section of the .js via @ param and then make a call inside the (function(){
(with the name specified name involved).
The engine has troubles with handing decimals when it comes to the UP and DOWN arrows. In my case, I set my @ max(arbitrarily) to 0.100 @ min to 0.001
(default of the engine).
So pressing the arrows worked as a binary, only switching between the two values. Thats okay, I wrote a memo in the @ desc box below to type it manually. Works that way.
@decimals 3 was needed or else it only displayed 0.
Thanks Robro33
(and the rest)!
Honestly, without looking up other plugins, Id have never guessed a large part of it all
(lots of parts make no sense to the untrained)
. Here's how the relevant part looks for those curious:
Spoiler:
Plugin's End.
Code:
* @param LuckValueBonus
 * @text Luck Value [% Bonus]
 * @desc Enter the bonus value per points of luck  differences.
 * The arrow buttons are broken: Type manually. Default values: O.OO1
 * @default 0.001
 * @type number
 * @decimals 3
 * @max 0.100
 * @min 0.001
 *
 */


(function() {
"use strict";

    const name = 'Luck Effect Booster';
    const parameters = PluginManager.parameters(name);
    let luckValueBonus = Number(parameters['LuckValueBonus'] || 0.001);


    Game_Action.prototype.lukEffectRate = function(target) {
    return Math.max(1.0 + (this.subject().luk - target.luk) * luckValueBonus, 0.0);
    };


})();
